WEBVTT

00:00:00.011 --> 00:00:04.497
For the first version of RAG, we only need a small set of libraries.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:08.097
Pandas will manage the document and chunk tables.

00:00:08.097 --> 00:00:11.807
NumPy will hold our vectors and help with ranking.

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:15.367
Sentence Transformers will create embeddings.

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:23.808
Scikit-learn is useful for similarity calculations, and the OpenAI Python client can connect to a language model later.

00:00:24.426 --> 00:00:26.718
Notice what we are not installing yet.

00:00:26.718 --> 00:00:33.798
We are not starting with several vector databases, LangChain, or a large orchestration framework.

00:00:33.798 --> 00:00:35.908
That is intentional.

00:00:35.908 --> 00:00:42.598
When too many libraries are introduced at the beginning, it becomes difficult to tell which part of the system is

00:00:42.598 --> 00:00:49.003
actually doing the retrieval. Start with the simplest environment that can demonstrate the architecture.

00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:56.468
Once that works, adding a vector database becomes a meaningful engineering choice rather than another mysterious

00:00:56.468 --> 00:00:57.163
package.
